{
   "id": "ITM-AGT-10009-05",
   "archetype_id": "BC-QA-10009",
   "variant_id": null,
   "format": "mcq",
   "stem": {
      "text": "A function \\( f \\) has derivatives of all orders. The first-degree Taylor polynomial for \\( f \\) about \\( x = 1 \\) is used to approximate \\( f\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right) \\). It is known that \\( f''(1) = 1 \\) and that \\( |f''(x)| \\le 4 \\) for all \\( x \\) in \\( \\left[1, \\frac{4}{3}\\right] \\). The approximation is meant to be within \\( \\frac{1}{2} \\) of \\( f\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right) \\). Find the Lagrange error bound for this approximation."
   },
   "answer_key": {
      "form": "symbolic",
      "mathjson": [
         "Rational",
         2,
         9
      ]
   },
   "worked_solution": [
      {
         "step": 1,
         "text": "A degree 1 polynomial needs the maximum of \\( |f''(x)| \\), the derivative of order \\( 2 \\), between the centre and the input.",
         "mathjson": 2
      },
      {
         "step": 2,
         "text": "The bound is \\( \\frac{M\\,|x - 1|^{2}}{2!} \\) with \\( M = 4 \\) and \\( |x - 1| = \\frac{1}{3} \\).",
         "mathjson": [
            "Rational",
            2,
            9
         ]
      },
      {
         "step": 3,
         "text": "Evaluating gives \\( \\frac{4 \\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)^{2}}{2} = \\frac{2}{9} \\).",
         "mathjson": [
            "Rational",
            2,
            9
         ]
      },
      {
         "step": 4,
         "text": "The error is at most \\( \\frac{2}{9} \\), which is at most \\( \\frac{1}{2} \\).",
         "mathjson": [
            "Rational",
            2,
            9
         ]
      }
   ],
